WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.903
- Good afternoon, President Salovey,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.690
Marta Moret, colleagues, and friends,

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.960
and welcome to the Tanner
Lecture on Human Values,

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.450
hosted by the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:24.573
My name is Alice Kaplan and
I'm director of the Whitney.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.200
One of the privileges and
pleasures at the Whitney

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.970
is to advise the president

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.790
on the selection of Tanner lecturers.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.418
This year, we've had the
exceptional opportunity

00:00:35.418 --> 00:00:38.130
to invite a speaker working in the field

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:40.230
of artificial intelligence.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.720
Fei-Fei Li is an international leader

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.420
in establishing a human-centered framework

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.630
for artificial intelligence in research,

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.840
education, practice, and policy.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.360
Her visit to Yale is especially meaningful

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:55.950
for us at the Whitney, of course,

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:59.490
because it's our first Tanner
Lecture since the pandemic

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.880
and our first here in the
humanities quadrangle,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.970
but also because it's
allowed us to reach deep

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.720
into our mission, to blend ways of knowing

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.660
as we've made contact with
a community of scholars

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.520
working in artificial intelligence

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.310
and related fields across the campus.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.950
We've said to many of you that we are new

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.470
to the neighborhood of
artificial intelligence

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.243
and delighted to be here.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.170
I'm especially grateful
to our Dean of the Faculty

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.020
of Arts and Sciences, Tamar Gendler,

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.720
to Gary Tomlinson, former Whitney Director

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:36.840
and Sterling Professor
of Music and Humanities,

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.710
and to Alan Gerber, Sterling
Professor of Political Science,

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.690
Director of the Institution for Social

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.343
and Public Policy Studies,
Professor of Economics

00:01:45.343 --> 00:01:47.850
and Statistics and Data Science

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.830
for their knowledge and their support

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.213
in planning this event.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.930
Before we begin, let me say a few words

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.450
about the Tanner Lectures on Human Values,

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.030
which were established
by the American scholar,

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.780
industrialist, and
philanthropist Obert Clark Tanner

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.630
and his wife, Grace Tanner.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.950
Educated at the University of
Utah, Stanford, and Harvard,

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.250
Obert Tanner went on to
become a New Testament scholar

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:17.130
and professor at the University of Utah,

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.770
but that is not the whole story.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.780
In order to pay for his education,

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:26.430
he founded a jewelry company
while still an undergraduate.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.920
His company, O.C. Tanner,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.980
which manufactures
employee recognition awards

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.620
and seeks to improve workplace cultures

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.200
through personalized employee recognition,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.870
became an extraordinary success,

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.760
and it is on this base that Grace

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.150
and Obert Tanner's philanthropy was built.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.660
Yale is one of a number
of universities in the US,

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:52.800
the UK, and beyond to
sponsor Tanner Lectures.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.010
Appointment as a Tanner
lecturer is a recognition

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.520
of uncommon achievement
and outstanding ability

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:01.890
in the field of human values.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.580
The lecturers come from many
fields and walks of life,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.030
philosophy, religion,
and the other humanities,

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:12.300
the sciences, the creative
arts, the learned professions,

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.620
or, indeed, from leadership
in public or private affairs.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.160
The lectureships are
international and intercultural

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.920
and are meant to transcend
ethnic, national,

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.790
religious, and ideological distinctions.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.980
As I noted earlier, at Yale,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.620
Tanner lecturers are
selected by the president

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.720
and consultation with the director

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.160
and the executive committee

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.440
of the Whitney Humanities Center.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.560
We are thrilled, though
not at all surprised,

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.210
by this big turnout.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.280
Yale is obviously delighted

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.280
that President Salovey invited Fei-Fei Li,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.680
Sequoia Professor of Computer Science

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:53.220
at Stanford University
and Denning Co-Director

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:57.570
of the Stanford Institute
for Human-Centered AI.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.820
You'll hear more about
Professor Li's achievements

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.400
and the impact of her work in a moment.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.980
Please note that there will be no question

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.770
and answer period following the lecture.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:09.960
The video recording will be posted

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.380
on the Yale YouTube
channel in the coming days,

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:14.940
and we invite you to check

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.100
the Whitney Humanity Center website

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:19.560
and subscribe to our weekly email

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.450
at subscribe.yale.edu for updates.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:26.160
Now it is my pleasure to welcome

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.010
to the podium Professor Marvin Chun,

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:32.190
Richard M. Colgate Professor of Psychology

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:34.470
and Professor of Neuroscience.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:37.200
Marvin, it's such a pleasure
after having worked with you

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.517
in your capacity as dean of the college

00:04:39.517 --> 00:04:42.840
to welcome you today as
a leader in the study

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.233
of brain imaging and
artificial intelligence.

00:04:48.642 --> 00:04:51.809
(audience applauding)

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.280
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.980
Dr. Fei-Fei Li is one
of the most important

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.160
and influential researchers
in the entire history

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.110
of artificial intelligence.

00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.600
If you enjoy the ability
to use verbal labels

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.780
to search through your
library of thousands of photos

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:13.320
or trust your Tesla to
maintain its lane and speed

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.530
as it races along with
other cars down a highway,

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:18.930
then you owe much to Dr. Li.

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.500
An AI system is only as good
as the data used to train it,

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.600
and amongst her many accomplishments,

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.690
she is responsible for
developing ImageNet,

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:29.370
a labeled database of images

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.770
that enabled revolutionary advances

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:35.730
in computer vision over
the past 10 to 15 years.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:37.980
Even though I'm not a computer scientist,

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.650
I'm deeply honored to
have this opportunity

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:44.700
to introduce Dr. Li because
of our shared background

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.470
in cognitive neuroscience,

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:49.950
especially scene perception
and categorization,

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:53.880
a topic to which Dr. Li has
made pioneering contributions,

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.730
even before developing ImageNet.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:06:00.690
Her broad research interests
span cognitively inspired AI,

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:02.760
machine learning, deep learning,

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:05.640
computer vision, and AI plus healthcare,

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:07.740
especially ambient intelligence systems

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:09.810
for healthcare delivery.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:13.050
Dr. Li has published more
than 200 scientific articles

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:16.380
in top-tier journals and
conference proceedings.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:19.560
Even more impressive is
her measurable impact

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:24.330
with a total of over 181,000 citations.

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:28.110
Her work attracts more
attention in one year

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:31.203
than most researchers gain
over their entire careers.

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:34.830
Not surprisingly, she has been recognized

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.540
with countless honors and awards,

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:40.260
including election to the
National Academy of Engineering,

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.090
the National Academy of Medicine,

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:45.270
and the American Academy
of Arts and Sciences.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:48.090
I not only admire her
accomplishments in research

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:50.430
but her contributions to society.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:52.560
She's a national leading voice

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.280
for advocating diversity in STEM and AI.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.250
After serving as a director
of Stanford's AI Lab,

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:01.320
she took a sabbatical
from Stanford to serve

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:04.530
as the vice president at
Google and as Chief Scientist

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:07.443
of AI/Machine Learning at Google Cloud.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:10.590
Now, Professor Li is the Co-Director

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:14.250
of Stanford Human-Centered AI Institute,

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:17.190
and beyond campus, she is
co-founder and chairperson

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:20.460
of the national nonprofit AI for All,

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:24.273
aimed at increasing inclusion
and diversity in AI education.

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:28.620
Recognized in 2016 by
the Carnegie Foundation

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:33.240
as one of the Great Immigrants:
The Pride of America,

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:36.330
if you consider other past
honorees on this list,

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:40.260
I can describe Dr. Li to
be like the Albert Einstein

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:43.113
or the Yo-Yo Ma of
artificial intelligence.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:47.097
We are excited to hear her
speak to us about "What We See

00:07:47.097 --> 00:07:50.760
and What We Value: AI
with a Human Perspective."

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.230
Please join me in welcoming

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:54.840
this year's Whitney Humanities
Center Tanner Lecturer

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.723
on Human Values, Dr. Fei-Fei Li.

00:07:57.723 --> 00:08:00.890
(audience applauding)

00:08:06.922 --> 00:08:10.440
- Thank you. It's quite
an honor to be here.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.559
I really want to thank President Salovey

00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:18.510
and the Whitney Humanities
Center for quite an honor.

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:21.030
Of course, I cannot pass the opportunity

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:24.900
to also honor another
incredible computer scientist

00:08:24.900 --> 00:08:26.100
who came out of Yale.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:28.080
That is Dr. Grace Hopper.

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:31.440
So for all of us, especially women,

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:35.910
working in computer science,
even after almost 90 years,

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:38.970
we still are following her footsteps

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:42.580
and trying to live up to
the incredible leadership

00:08:43.676 --> 00:08:48.300
and trailblazing careers
she has established

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:50.550
for all the women in computer science,

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:54.330
and that journey has been
long and will still continue.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:57.870
We haven't get to where
she probably envisioned

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.630
for all of us, but it's
quite an honor to be back

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:03.690
to the alma mater of Dr. Grace Hopper.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:07.290
So I'm here to really share with you

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:11.580
some of my own work in the field of AI,

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:15.660
and I have to confess, as a
scientist and technologist,

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:18.870
when I enter this incredibly exciting

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:23.870
but not really new field
in AI two-plus decades ago,

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.280
it would not have been my wildest dream

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:29.940
that how profound this field has become

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:34.410
to the daily lives of
our collective humanity

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:35.850
and our society.

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:38.670
So I really cherish this moment

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:40.770
to share with you what we have learned

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:42.870
but really hope to engage

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:46.140
in a productive conversation with you.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:49.710
I understand there will be a
conversation tomorrow as well

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:52.470
and hope to meet some of
you after the lecture.

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:54.030
So thank you so much.

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:55.383
I'll begin my talk.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:57.870
As Alice has said,

00:09:57.870 --> 00:10:02.130
the topic of my talk today is "What We See

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:06.960
and What We Value: AI
with a Human Perspective."

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.000
I'm going to take you back in history,

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.480
a little bit back in history,

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:15.123
540 million years ago.

00:10:16.392 --> 00:10:18.870
(laughs) Just a little while back.

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:21.270
Life on Earth was very different.

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:23.850
It was all underwater.

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:27.390
Animals live in the
primordial soup of the ocean,

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:30.897
and frankly, there aren't that
many animals in the world.

00:10:30.897 --> 00:10:32.490
And life was very chill.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:34.530
They float around.
(audience chuckling)

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.980
And then something really
remarkable happened.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:38.490
It was a mystery.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:42.840
In what geologically consider
a very short period of time,

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:46.950
10 million years, between
540 million years ago

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:51.360
to 530 million years ago,
fossil studies have told us

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:55.200
the number of animal
species just exploded.

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:56.520
What happened?

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.290
There were many theories,

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:00.870
from climate change to
the chemical composition

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:03.360
of the ocean water and all this.

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:07.110
But there is one influential conjecture

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:10.680
that has captured the attention,
and this was put forth

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:14.280
by a Australian zoologist
called Andrew Parker.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:15.900
By studying fossils,

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:19.500
he conjectured that
the Cambrian Explosion,

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:22.170
by the way, that's what
the period was called

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:25.710
with the animal speciation,
Cambrian Explosion

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:28.140
or Big Bang of Evolution, actually,

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:31.740
the Cambrian Explosion is triggered

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:34.590
by the sudden evolution of vision,

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:38.100
which set off an evolutionary arms race

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:41.433
where animals either evolved or died.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:46.170
So vision, the onset of
vision and perception,

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:48.180
was one of the driving forces

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:51.090
of the evolution of animal species.

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:53.640
Furthermore, it's a driving force

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:56.970
of the evolution of the nervous system,

00:11:56.970 --> 00:12:01.800
eventually leading up to very
intelligent animals like us.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.440
In fact, today on Earth, most,

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:08.010
the majority of the animals
uses visual perception

00:12:08.010 --> 00:12:10.140
as a major sensory system

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:12.840
to guide its survival

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:15.720
and thriving of the species.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:20.310
And for us humans, we're
very much visual animals.

00:12:20.310 --> 00:12:24.150
Our visual intelligence
gets us to communicate

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:27.540
with each other, to work,
to navigate the world,

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:31.680
to interact with the world,
to entertain ourselves,

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.830
and it's hard to believe

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:39.750
that we can live without much
of the visual intelligence.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.310
In the meantime,

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:45.300
the field of intelligence
also started to evolve

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.090
in addition to what natural evolution is

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:50.130
in the field of computer science.

00:12:50.130 --> 00:12:55.080
AI compared to evolution is a
very, very, very young field.

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:58.020
Most people say it's about 60 years.

00:12:58.020 --> 00:12:59.910
But the history of computer vision

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:03.840
and the history of AI
is very much entangled.

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:06.960
Just at the very dawn of AI,

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.513
back around 60 years ago,

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:14.580
there was a very ambitious
MIT summer project,

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:19.177
and the MIT summer project
proposed by this professor says,

00:13:19.177 --> 00:13:21.420
"We'll use our summer workers,"

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:24.007
and I suppose they had some smart ones,

00:13:24.007 --> 00:13:28.500
"effectively to construct
a significant part

00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:32.250
of a visual system or a
artificial visual system

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:33.990
within one summer."

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:36.870
Well, that didn't quite happen,

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:40.560
but the urban legend goes
that this is the beginning

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:42.660
of the field of computer vision,

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:45.720
that someone or a collection

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:49.680
of pioneering computer
scientists had to believe

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:54.680
that one day, we can create a
artificial intelligence system

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:59.820
with the kind of visual
intelligence that humans enjoy.

00:13:59.820 --> 00:14:03.390
Fast forward to the second decade

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:07.020
or actually the third
decade of 21st century.

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:09.150
Vision has come a long way.

00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:12.150
Just as Marvin was mentioning
about self-driving cars

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:16.440
or visual driving assistance

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:18.690
to image classification

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:22.110
to the incredible work

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:25.200
recently by generative arts,

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:28.740
vision has not only become a blossoming,

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:31.830
a vibrant academic discipline,

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:34.320
but it also has become a driving force

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:37.800
in commercialization and industry.

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:41.640
So the rest of the talk is
really to share with you

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:45.720
my own journey and
perspective about my field.

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:49.560
Where have we come from
and where are we heading?

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:53.220
And also, what are the human lessons

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:55.590
that we have learned along the way

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:58.020
of developing computer vision?

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:00.480
So one thing that's been really fun

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:03.180
to hear about this
audience is that you come

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:06.270
from all walks of discipline and areas,

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:09.450
and I'm not going to assume
everybody's a computer scientist

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:11.730
or a student of AI,

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:15.690
so let me just give a
very simple definition,

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:19.740
because a lot of you probably
hear the words like AI,

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:22.410
machine learning, deep
learning, computer vision, NLP.

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:23.760
What are these things?

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:26.280
Well, here's a cheat sheet.
(audience chuckling)

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:29.493
I'm not going to go over
everything and I won't quiz you,

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:33.810
but let's just get some basic definitions.

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:35.550
Artificial intelligence, AI,

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:39.180
is actually a term coined
by computer scientist

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:42.750
and Stanford's Emeritus
Professor John McCarthy.

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:45.273
Back in the 1950s, early '60s,

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:49.260
he described this field as the science

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:53.520
and engineering of making
intelligent machines.

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:56.730
Nature took 540 million years.

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:58.830
Let's see how long that
would take humanity

00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:02.610
to create a silicon-based
intelligent machine.

00:16:02.610 --> 00:16:04.980
And within the field of
artificial intelligence,

00:16:04.980 --> 00:16:06.600
especially as it grows,

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:10.350
you start to see areas
of disciplines emerge,

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:12.870
such as field of computer vision,

00:16:12.870 --> 00:16:15.210
field of natural language processing,

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:18.960
speech recognition,
and robotics and so on,

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:21.030
and there are more and more newer fields

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:22.770
within artificial intelligence

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:25.530
or related to artificial intelligence.

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:26.363
Okay.

00:16:26.363 --> 00:16:27.570
And you also hear terms

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:29.370
like machine learning and deep learning.

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:30.750
What are these things?

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:34.410
Well, consider machine learning

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:38.730
the mathematical language
of doing today's AI.

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:41.310
In fact, there are other
mathematical languages

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:44.820
have been developed in the history of AI,

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:47.490
but for the purpose of this talk,

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:52.260
machine learning is a
relatively young discipline

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:55.170
in computer science that
combines statistics,

00:16:55.170 --> 00:16:58.620
apply math, and even inspired
by neuroscience and psychology

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:02.820
and all this in terms of
creating computer programs

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:07.200
based on statistical
models to achieve the goals

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:10.470
of different kinds of AI tasks.

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:12.330
And within machine learning,

00:17:12.330 --> 00:17:15.660
the latest revolution is based on a family

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:18.630
of algorithms called neural networks.

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:20.760
And we'll get to that a little more later,

00:17:20.760 --> 00:17:23.610
but you hear neural networks a lot

00:17:23.610 --> 00:17:25.380
probably on news these days,

00:17:25.380 --> 00:17:29.820
and also there is a better name now

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:32.340
or less nerdy name for neural network,

00:17:32.340 --> 00:17:33.690
which is called deep learning.

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:36.630
So these are the words
you will hear from me.

00:17:36.630 --> 00:17:39.570
Okay, so let's get to the talk.

00:17:39.570 --> 00:17:42.990
So I want to share with you three parts

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:44.910
of the work we have done,

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:49.023
and the first one is building
AI to see what humans see.

00:17:50.070 --> 00:17:52.680
First of all, let's just accept this.

00:17:52.680 --> 00:17:57.120
Humans are incredible visual
animals, and we can see a lot.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:00.240
In fact, our visual system is very robust.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:02.940
This is a seminal study by Molly Potter

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:07.560
back in the 1970s with her colleagues

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:10.050
to show you that...

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:12.750
She show you a video and consider you

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:16.740
a human subject in her
lab, and nod your head

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:21.030
if you see a person in one of the frames.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:21.960
Okay, I see all.

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:24.150
Good. You have good visual systems.

00:18:24.150 --> 00:18:28.320
We get that out of the way, and...

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:31.590
But take a moment to reflect
how remarkable this is.

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:35.490
Every frame here is only on the screen

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:39.660
for a hundred millisecond,
refreshed at 10 hertz frequency.

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:43.440
And other than, say, me
telling you to detect a person,

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:45.330
you don't know what the person looks like,

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:48.660
the gesture of the person,
where in the frame it is,

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:50.790
what kind of clothes it's wearing.

00:18:50.790 --> 00:18:53.310
You don't know if this is
a man, woman, or anything.

00:18:53.310 --> 00:18:56.700
Yet, your visual system is really quick

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:59.850
at recognizing and detecting this object.

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:02.670
Furthermore, by the turn of the century,

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:04.980
shortly before the turn of the century,

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:07.140
neurophysiologists like Simon Thorpe

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:10.500
and his colleagues have also put a speed

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:14.130
of processing of our
remarkable visual system

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:17.400
when categorizing complex natural scenes.

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:19.890
The experiment he did
is to put human subject

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:22.620
in front of computers and flash them

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:26.250
with complex photos of
animals or non-animals.

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:27.630
Here are some examples.

00:19:27.630 --> 00:19:29.917
And the task of the human is to just say

00:19:29.917 --> 00:19:33.150
"Yes, I see an animal" or
"I don't see an animal."

00:19:33.150 --> 00:19:35.820
Mind you, these are really
complex real-life pictures.

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:38.160
You don't know which kind of
animal is going to come up.

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:41.580
You don't know what the distractor
pictures are going to be.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:42.450
And in the meantime,

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:45.630
he is measuring your
brain wave through EEG.

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:48.720
And as early as 150 millisecond

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:51.720
after the onset of the visual stimuli,

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:55.950
he sees a differential
signal in human brain

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:57.780
that tells you the difference

00:19:57.780 --> 00:20:01.140
between an animal picture
and a non-animal picture.

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:05.100
Considering our wetware
here is a lot slower

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:08.100
than transistors, this is
a remarkable achievement

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:10.560
in terms of the fleeting speed

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:13.110
that our visual processing is.

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:16.440
In addition to temporal capabilities,

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:20.940
we also find neural
correlates, areas in the brain,

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:23.850
that are dedicated to object recognition,

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:28.050
such as faces or places
or even body parts.

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:30.750
This is a concurrent set of studies

00:20:30.750 --> 00:20:32.910
during the turn of the century

00:20:32.910 --> 00:20:36.210
showing us these kind of brain areas.

00:20:36.210 --> 00:20:39.330
So all this is to show you

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:41.400
that there's something remarkable

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:44.070
about our human visual brain,

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:48.360
and one of the most remarkable
thing is our ability

00:20:48.360 --> 00:20:53.070
to do object recognition
or to categorize objects.

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:57.000
In fact, we believe, and
cognitive neuroscientists

00:20:57.000 --> 00:20:59.310
and vision psychologists believe,

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:02.040
object recognition is a
fundamental building block

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:03.870
of visual intelligence.

00:21:03.870 --> 00:21:06.840
And for those of us who work in AI,

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:10.140
especially trying to make machines see,

00:21:10.140 --> 00:21:12.240
this has become a north star.

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:14.460
It's a important ability we need

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:16.860
to endow the machines with.

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:18.780
So formally speaking,

00:21:18.780 --> 00:21:23.370
the task of object recognition
is to show, let's say,

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:27.570
an agent, whether it's
a a brain or a computer,

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:32.250
a picture of an object,
and the agent is capable

00:21:32.250 --> 00:21:37.250
of output the category of
what this picture contains,

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:41.043
in this case, a very cute
Australian animal, a wombat.

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:46.710
You might wonder how hard is
it because you can all do it.

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:49.500
Well, mathematically speaking,

00:21:49.500 --> 00:21:53.880
any 3D object in the world
can render infinite number

00:21:53.880 --> 00:21:56.250
of pictures in a 2D frame,

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:59.880
whether it's a camera
frame or your retina,

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:04.770
because of the infinite
possibilities of lighting,

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:08.460
appearance differences,
background clutter, occlusion,

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:11.550
self-occlusion, camera viewing angles.

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:14.010
So this is a hard task,

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:17.040
and now evolution has solved it for us.

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:19.860
Let's see how we solve it in computers.

00:22:19.860 --> 00:22:21.420
At this point, I'm going to bring you

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:23.350
to the last lesson

00:22:24.510 --> 00:22:29.490
or diversion of machine
learning and AI today.

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:32.010
It's this concept of generalization.

00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:33.930
So for the rest of the talk,

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:36.390
no matter what project I'm talking about,

00:22:36.390 --> 00:22:39.120
as long as I'm talking about AI,

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:42.450
mathematically, the goal of AI

00:22:42.450 --> 00:22:44.910
and developing a AI algorithm

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:47.730
is to solve the problem of generalization.

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:49.950
What is the problem of generalization?

00:22:49.950 --> 00:22:52.890
The generalization is the following.

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:55.720
When we develop an algorithm or a system

00:22:57.464 --> 00:22:59.340
that is capable of doing something,

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:03.600
in this case, recognizing
objects or wombat pictures,

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:08.600
our goal is actually not
to train the algorithm

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:11.763
so that the training
error goes to minimal.

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:15.330
That's a little counterintuitive
because of course

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:17.940
you want the training error
to go to minimal, right?

00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:19.940
That's why you send your kids to school.

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:25.680
But your kids will also get out of school

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:27.180
and go to the real world,

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:29.310
so what you really want is,

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:33.630
once the algorithm encounter a case,

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:35.790
an example that is not in training

00:23:35.790 --> 00:23:39.600
but in testing or in generalization time,

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:43.830
you want the algorithm
still to be able to do well.

00:23:43.830 --> 00:23:47.220
You want the testing error
or the generalization error

00:23:47.220 --> 00:23:48.690
to be as small as possible.

00:23:48.690 --> 00:23:51.930
In fact, the gap between the testing error

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:53.010
and the training error,

00:23:53.010 --> 00:23:55.560
you want it to be as small as possible.

00:23:55.560 --> 00:23:58.800
And that goal or that process

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:02.520
of making this happen is generalization.

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:05.970
And in AI or machine learning,
our mathematical goal

00:24:05.970 --> 00:24:10.110
is always to achieve the
best possible generalization.

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:12.900
Okay, we got the nerd talk out of the way.

00:24:12.900 --> 00:24:16.770
So let's continue with the
visual tasks we care about,

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:18.660
which is object recognition.

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:21.630
In the early days, back last century,

00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:25.650
the pioneering computer
scientist have used,

00:24:25.650 --> 00:24:27.360
there was very little compute,

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:29.337
there was practically no data,

00:24:29.337 --> 00:24:32.460
and we know very little about
the mathematical models.

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:35.370
So pioneering scientists have designed

00:24:35.370 --> 00:24:38.670
hand-designed features
and hand-designed models

00:24:38.670 --> 00:24:40.260
to try to account

00:24:40.260 --> 00:24:44.460
for how to recognize complex objects.

00:24:44.460 --> 00:24:47.610
And it was a beautiful...

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:50.580
It was a bunch of beautiful
conceptual theories,

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:54.300
but it didn't go very far in
real-world generalization.

00:24:54.300 --> 00:24:58.290
Around the turn of 20th to 21st century,

00:24:58.290 --> 00:25:00.510
a new tool emerged,

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:03.690
and that fundamentally
changed many areas of AI,

00:25:03.690 --> 00:25:05.850
including computer vision,
and that's the tool

00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:08.460
of machine learning or
statistical modeling.

00:25:08.460 --> 00:25:12.840
So in this time, we start to
see a lot of interesting work

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:16.740
of using hand-designed
features or patches of images

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:19.170
but start to use machine learning models

00:25:19.170 --> 00:25:22.890
to learn the parameters of these features.

00:25:22.890 --> 00:25:25.800
This might be a little over
the head for some of you

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:28.170
who are not in computer
science, but it doesn't matter.

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:30.450
All you need to know
is that we're starting

00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:33.360
to move towards machine learning

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:37.770
and machine-learned
parameterization of models.

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:40.743
Something else important
happened during that time,

00:25:43.215 --> 00:25:46.830
which is actually the
availability of data.

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:48.600
Internet start to happen,

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:51.720
and the field of AI and
computer vision start

00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:56.640
to have available pictures
to train our algorithms on.

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:59.070
So in this seminal early work

00:25:59.070 --> 00:26:01.200
by a group of European scientists,

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:04.290
they created this data
set called PASCAL VOC

00:26:04.290 --> 00:26:08.250
so that the whole community
of computer vision field

00:26:08.250 --> 00:26:12.060
can start working on the
object recognition problem

00:26:12.060 --> 00:26:16.380
by using this data set
of a few thousand images

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:19.470
and 20 object classes.

00:26:19.470 --> 00:26:22.650
That was also around the
time I graduated from PhD

00:26:22.650 --> 00:26:25.650
and became an assistant professor,

00:26:25.650 --> 00:26:28.200
and something really bugged me,

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:31.380
because while it's really cool,

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:33.720
we're working on this north star problem

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:38.400
of object recognition, but
we're working with 20 objects.

00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:41.430
Cognitive psychologists have told us

00:26:41.430 --> 00:26:43.590
humans have a remarkable ability

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:46.230
of recognizing object categories,

00:26:46.230 --> 00:26:51.150
more than 30,000 object
categories by tender age of six.

00:26:51.150 --> 00:26:53.940
So 20 versus 30,000,

00:26:53.940 --> 00:26:56.520
it didn't fully compute.

00:26:56.520 --> 00:27:01.520
So my students and I started
to examine the previous work

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:04.350
in color vision and object recognition.

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:07.050
I realized one of the biggest problem

00:27:07.050 --> 00:27:12.000
we might be facing is not the
hand-design models per se.

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:14.610
It's actually the data we're training on.

00:27:14.610 --> 00:27:17.550
So in a three-year effort,

00:27:17.550 --> 00:27:21.900
my collaborators and my students
and I went a little crazy.

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:25.589
We downloaded the entire
internet we could get to

00:27:25.589 --> 00:27:28.140
back in 2006, 2007.

00:27:28.140 --> 00:27:31.890
More than a billion images we hand-curated

00:27:31.890 --> 00:27:34.620
through a crowd engineering method.

00:27:34.620 --> 00:27:38.910
15 meticulously cleaned
and labeled images...

00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:39.743
Oh, sorry. (laughing)
(audience laughing)

00:27:39.743 --> 00:27:44.570
15 million meticulously
curated, labeled images

00:27:45.450 --> 00:27:48.630
across 22,000 object categories.

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:52.380
And that was the ImageNet project.

00:27:52.380 --> 00:27:56.250
And ImageNet was born

00:27:56.250 --> 00:27:58.440
with the ambition

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:01.950
that it will fuel computer vision

00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:03.930
and machine learning algorithm

00:28:03.930 --> 00:28:07.830
in a fundamentally,
qualitatively different way.

00:28:07.830 --> 00:28:11.400
And to our, I wouldn't say surprise,

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:15.840
but we're very happy to
see that start to happen.

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:20.310
A particular family of
model that did really well

00:28:20.310 --> 00:28:24.660
when there is a huge flood
and wealth of data available

00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:27.960
is neural network models or algorithms,

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:31.620
especially in computer vision,
convolutional neural network.

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:33.930
Convolutional neural network is one

00:28:33.930 --> 00:28:38.280
of the statistical models
developed since the last century,

00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:42.210
and it's not a particularly
new set of algorithms.

00:28:42.210 --> 00:28:46.710
The early onset of the
convolutional neural network history

00:28:46.710 --> 00:28:49.860
began even in the '60s and '70s

00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:51.900
and was further refined by people

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:53.937
like Jeff Hinton and Yann LeCun.

00:28:53.937 --> 00:28:55.680
And for those of you not familiar

00:28:55.680 --> 00:28:57.540
with this neural network model,

00:28:57.540 --> 00:29:00.420
it is actually inspired
by the brain, in fact,

00:29:00.420 --> 00:29:03.690
by the cat brain and the
Hubel and Wiesel study

00:29:03.690 --> 00:29:06.450
on the cat visual system.

00:29:06.450 --> 00:29:09.180
So the fundamental building block

00:29:09.180 --> 00:29:11.790
of a neural network is a node,

00:29:11.790 --> 00:29:15.030
a neuron-like node that takes input

00:29:15.030 --> 00:29:17.880
from other nodes and sends output.

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:20.580
And not only they take inputs,

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:23.040
they also are layered hierarchically

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:27.240
so you can pass information
around in a efficient way.

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:30.180
So even 10 years ago,

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:34.620
with the availability of
ImageNet, we were making models,

00:29:34.620 --> 00:29:38.292
neural network models as
large as millions of nodes

00:29:38.292 --> 00:29:40.740
and hundreds of millions of parameters

00:29:40.740 --> 00:29:42.240
and billions of connections.

00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:47.240
And this number is tiny compared
to today's neural network,

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:50.610
orders of magnitude
smaller than what we see

00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:53.100
in today's computer science.

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:56.400
So with the help

00:29:56.400 --> 00:30:01.400
of ImageNet and the
neural network algorithm,

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:03.600
the field of computer vision started

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:07.320
to see success in object recognition.

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:11.430
So we're able to categorize,
say, a picture of cat

00:30:11.430 --> 00:30:15.250
or even find a cat in a
more natural everyday photo

00:30:20.820 --> 00:30:23.910
or some other pictures, some other objects

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:27.450
such as teddy bears or girls or a boy

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:31.200
or even small objects like dog and kite.

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:34.980
And then a historical moment happened.

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:36.250
So for three years

00:30:38.159 --> 00:30:41.010
after we build ImageNet,

00:30:41.010 --> 00:30:43.680
we actually open-source this data set.

00:30:43.680 --> 00:30:48.680
We believe this is an important
asset to our community.

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:50.820
Not only we open-sourced it,

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:53.370
we created an international challenge

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:56.100
to invite researchers around the world

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:59.220
to participate in the object
recognition challenge.

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:03.510
And we curated a particular task

00:31:03.510 --> 00:31:06.480
called the ImageNet
object classification task

00:31:06.480 --> 00:31:10.590
with a thousand object classes
and millions of images.

00:31:10.590 --> 00:31:12.390
And then in 2012,

00:31:12.390 --> 00:31:16.230
the winning algorithm of
the ImageNet challenge

00:31:16.230 --> 00:31:19.080
is by Professor Jeff
Hinton and his student

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:21.870
called ImageNet classification

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:24.720
with deep convolutional neural networks.

00:31:24.720 --> 00:31:28.590
With this work, and people
casually call it the AlexNet

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:33.120
because of the first author,
AlexNet cut the error rate,

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:36.153
or the generalization
error, as you all learned,

00:31:37.380 --> 00:31:42.380
significantly compared to
the previous years' models.

00:31:43.230 --> 00:31:44.760
And many people,

00:31:44.760 --> 00:31:48.030
and maybe historians will
tell us in the future,

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:52.800
many people now consider
this moment and this work,

00:31:52.800 --> 00:31:55.680
the ImageNet challenge and the Alex model,

00:31:55.680 --> 00:31:58.203
the beginning of the
deep learning revolution.

00:31:59.520 --> 00:32:02.160
But AlexNet is only the beginning.

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:04.770
There has been incredible progress

00:32:04.770 --> 00:32:07.560
in the field of computer vision,

00:32:07.560 --> 00:32:10.780
especially through these annual winners

00:32:11.635 --> 00:32:14.970
of ImageNet challenge
in the following years.

00:32:14.970 --> 00:32:17.610
And that trend didn't stop.

00:32:17.610 --> 00:32:21.390
ImageNet challenge and the
deep learning revolution

00:32:21.390 --> 00:32:25.380
took the world by a storm,
and we see the progress

00:32:25.380 --> 00:32:28.140
of computer vision just accelerating.

00:32:28.140 --> 00:32:31.050
And we're very honored
that ImageNet has been one

00:32:31.050 --> 00:32:35.070
of the most cited work in the
history of computer science,

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:37.893
and also, it spilled over.

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:40.800
Computer vision, as well as AI,

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:45.800
was no longer a niche academic
discipline since the 2012.

00:32:46.170 --> 00:32:49.980
It became a driving force of industry,

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:54.030
a transformative era

00:32:54.030 --> 00:32:58.797
of some people call the
fourth industrial revolution

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:02.370
or the deep learning revolution,

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:05.760
and the field of computer vision,

00:33:05.760 --> 00:33:07.650
if you look at some market research,

00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:10.770
there is projected tens
of billions of dollars

00:33:10.770 --> 00:33:13.380
of market available now
for computer vision,

00:33:13.380 --> 00:33:16.230
and from Tel Aviv to Silicon Valley,

00:33:16.230 --> 00:33:19.140
we're just continue to see the blossoming

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:23.820
and flourishing of
computer vision startups.

00:33:23.820 --> 00:33:25.830
So back in the lab,

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:28.410
my students and I don't believe

00:33:28.410 --> 00:33:31.140
that recognizing or the ability

00:33:31.140 --> 00:33:33.690
to label object classes is the end

00:33:33.690 --> 00:33:36.030
of our visual intelligence, right?

00:33:36.030 --> 00:33:37.770
In fact, here's a simple example.

00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:41.490
If I have two pictures
and they're both labeled

00:33:41.490 --> 00:33:45.240
with the ImageNet
classifier person and llama,

00:33:45.240 --> 00:33:48.810
you would think the second
picture is more or less

00:33:48.810 --> 00:33:51.810
telling the same story
as the first picture.

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:55.350
But the truth is there's a
lot more in the pixel space.

00:33:55.350 --> 00:33:58.410
In fact, this is a very different scene

00:33:58.410 --> 00:34:00.900
from the left one.

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:04.200
And psychologists and
cognitive scientists again knew

00:34:04.200 --> 00:34:07.170
about this before computer scientists.

00:34:07.170 --> 00:34:08.370
This is a friend of mine

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:11.340
and one of my favorite
papers by Jeremy Wolfe,

00:34:11.340 --> 00:34:13.803
partially because the
paper is only two pages.

00:34:14.700 --> 00:34:18.030
He had conjectured

00:34:18.030 --> 00:34:20.760
that just recognizing object is not enough

00:34:20.760 --> 00:34:23.100
to understand and categorize a scene.

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:26.940
Relationships between
objects must be coded.

00:34:26.940 --> 00:34:31.200
Indeed, there is rich
relationship within a scene

00:34:31.200 --> 00:34:33.840
beyond just the identity of objects.

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:35.670
So my students and collaborators

00:34:35.670 --> 00:34:38.520
and I actually propose this new work

00:34:38.520 --> 00:34:41.130
called scene graph representation,

00:34:41.130 --> 00:34:45.059
where we encode objects,
here in pink boxes,

00:34:45.059 --> 00:34:49.230
the pairwise relationships,
here in green boxes,

00:34:49.230 --> 00:34:52.200
as well as the attributes
that defines the objects,

00:34:52.200 --> 00:34:53.790
here in purple boxes.

00:34:53.790 --> 00:34:57.570
And you can see a simple picture
like this has a very rich

00:34:57.570 --> 00:35:00.990
and dense visual scene representation.

00:35:00.990 --> 00:35:03.340
And by creating a large data set

00:35:06.169 --> 00:35:08.580
with this kind of annotated data,

00:35:08.580 --> 00:35:13.580
we're able to do things like
predicting object relationships

00:35:13.680 --> 00:35:17.640
in a scene, in this case,
such as person riding a horse

00:35:17.640 --> 00:35:19.650
or person wearing a hat.

00:35:19.650 --> 00:35:23.820
In fact, scene graph representation
is very compositional,

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:26.220
and because of this, we can take advantage

00:35:26.220 --> 00:35:28.680
of the compositional nature and be able

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:31.320
to do something like zero shot learning,

00:35:31.320 --> 00:35:34.740
which is we don't have to
train every relationship

00:35:34.740 --> 00:35:36.540
with a ton of images.

00:35:36.540 --> 00:35:39.090
In this case, our algorithm was able

00:35:39.090 --> 00:35:41.550
to recognize horse wearing hat,

00:35:41.550 --> 00:35:44.643
which is a rare event in life,

00:35:45.510 --> 00:35:49.140
or person sitting on fire
hydrant, not so rare.

00:35:49.140 --> 00:35:51.990
And in general, our algorithm at that time

00:35:51.990 --> 00:35:55.230
using scene graph
representation performed better

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:58.680
than state-of-the-art
algorithm at that time.

00:35:58.680 --> 00:36:01.830
Of course, calling out
or labeling relationships

00:36:01.830 --> 00:36:03.690
is not the end of the story.

00:36:03.690 --> 00:36:08.220
In fact, we humans can
actually tell a visual story

00:36:08.220 --> 00:36:10.680
when we are presented with a scene.

00:36:10.680 --> 00:36:14.280
So around a couple of years ago,

00:36:14.280 --> 00:36:16.230
using deep learning algorithm,

00:36:16.230 --> 00:36:19.530
my students and collaborators
and I had done a series

00:36:19.530 --> 00:36:23.250
of work pushing the boundary
of visual recognition

00:36:23.250 --> 00:36:26.280
beyond objects, beyond
relationship predictions

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:28.950
into storytelling and captioning.

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:33.321
In this case, we showed
this picture to a computer,

00:36:33.321 --> 00:36:37.350
and the computers
automatically generate a story

00:36:37.350 --> 00:36:42.120
such as a man riding a horse-drawn
carriage down the street.

00:36:42.120 --> 00:36:44.400
And if we push it harder,

00:36:44.400 --> 00:36:48.870
they can write denser captions
or even paragraph captions.

00:36:48.870 --> 00:36:53.340
Even furthermore, the
visual world is not static,

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:57.090
and most of the world
is moving and dynamic.

00:36:57.090 --> 00:36:59.910
So we push the scene graph representation

00:36:59.910 --> 00:37:03.570
into spatial-temporal
space and we start to work

00:37:03.570 --> 00:37:05.910
on recognizing multi-object,

00:37:05.910 --> 00:37:09.420
multi-actor activities and relationships.

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:12.300
This is an example of an algorithm

00:37:12.300 --> 00:37:16.320
recognizing not only ping-pong
or table tennis players,

00:37:16.320 --> 00:37:19.590
but their movements and the relationship

00:37:19.590 --> 00:37:23.340
between the table, the ball,
the referee, and so on.

00:37:23.340 --> 00:37:25.482
So all this is to show you

00:37:25.482 --> 00:37:28.860
that the field of computer
vision has really blossomed

00:37:28.860 --> 00:37:32.040
and there are so many
interesting things to working.

00:37:32.040 --> 00:37:36.690
Beyond my own lab, our field
has made tremendous progress

00:37:36.690 --> 00:37:41.690
in 3D vision, in pose estimation,
in instance segmentation,

00:37:41.931 --> 00:37:45.840
and in generative art of vision.

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:47.610
And this journey hasn't stopped.

00:37:47.610 --> 00:37:50.820
We continue to see the explosion

00:37:50.820 --> 00:37:53.040
of computer vision development.

00:37:53.040 --> 00:37:57.960
So for this part, we have observed data,

00:37:57.960 --> 00:38:01.170
compute, and neural network algorithms

00:38:01.170 --> 00:38:05.430
ushered in the deep
learning revolution in AI.

00:38:05.430 --> 00:38:07.410
But I'm a firm believer

00:38:07.410 --> 00:38:11.520
that AI's development has
been and will always continue

00:38:11.520 --> 00:38:16.410
to be inspired by brain science
and human cognitive science.

00:38:16.410 --> 00:38:20.370
And here are the collaborators
and students and postdocs

00:38:20.370 --> 00:38:24.330
who have contributed to the
work I mentioned earlier.

00:38:24.330 --> 00:38:27.960
Now let's look beyond what humans can see.

00:38:27.960 --> 00:38:29.310
I know what humans can see

00:38:29.310 --> 00:38:31.620
as a inspiration of computer vision,

00:38:31.620 --> 00:38:34.863
but can we build AI to
see what humans don't see?

00:38:36.000 --> 00:38:39.120
Well, to start with,

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:41.880
we don't know everything
about the object world.

00:38:41.880 --> 00:38:45.120
In fact, can you name
all the dinosaurs here?

00:38:45.120 --> 00:38:48.330
Maybe if you have kids,
but even with kids,

00:38:48.330 --> 00:38:51.360
I'm unable to name all the dinosaurs,

00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:55.560
and there are tens of
thousands of bird species

00:38:55.560 --> 00:38:57.600
and thousands of car,

00:38:57.600 --> 00:39:01.020
I guess we don't call species, car types.

00:39:01.020 --> 00:39:03.420
And so this is what we call

00:39:03.420 --> 00:39:05.640
fine-grain object categorization,

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:08.640
and to be honest, most
humans, normal humans,

00:39:08.640 --> 00:39:12.120
cannot recognize that level

00:39:12.120 --> 00:39:15.840
of bird species or that many cars.

00:39:15.840 --> 00:39:17.760
And this is where computer algorithm

00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:21.510
have pushed the boundary
of human visual ability,

00:39:21.510 --> 00:39:24.690
and we have in our lab, as
well as other people's lab,

00:39:24.690 --> 00:39:27.300
created algorithms to do this kind

00:39:27.300 --> 00:39:30.360
of thousands of bird species recognition.

00:39:30.360 --> 00:39:32.370
And this is one of my favorite work

00:39:32.370 --> 00:39:36.930
because we scrape the
Craigslist or Kelley Blue Book

00:39:36.930 --> 00:39:41.160
to learn 2,800 types of cars

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:45.240
ever manufactured since the late,

00:39:45.240 --> 00:39:49.980
I think, 1970s, and then we
take these car detectors,

00:39:49.980 --> 00:39:53.400
we scanned Google Street View images

00:39:53.400 --> 00:39:56.130
across 200 American cities,

00:39:56.130 --> 00:39:58.080
and we learned about neighborhoods.

00:39:58.080 --> 00:39:59.760
We learned about sociology.

00:39:59.760 --> 00:40:03.090
We can correlate the recognition

00:40:03.090 --> 00:40:06.300
of cars with census data

00:40:06.300 --> 00:40:09.480
like neighborhood education level

00:40:09.480 --> 00:40:13.050
or income or even voting patterns.

00:40:13.050 --> 00:40:16.020
So computer vision,
just like human vision,

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:20.130
is a lens not only to
just see what the world is

00:40:20.130 --> 00:40:25.130
but to see and understand who
we are and the human society.

00:40:25.920 --> 00:40:28.470
So that was object categorization,

00:40:28.470 --> 00:40:30.150
but I want to push you a little bit

00:40:30.150 --> 00:40:35.150
because our human vision
system actually do have limits.

00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:39.000
And this is a famous visual illusion test

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:41.340
that many of you know, the Stroop test,

00:40:41.340 --> 00:40:43.804
and you know the words,

00:40:43.804 --> 00:40:47.520
but can you read to yourself
the color of the words,

00:40:47.520 --> 00:40:48.900
not the words itself?

00:40:48.900 --> 00:40:52.500
So from left to right and top to bottom,

00:40:52.500 --> 00:40:54.900
I would say red...

00:40:54.900 --> 00:40:56.010
Oh, God. (laughing)
(audience laughing)

00:40:56.010 --> 00:40:59.760
Red, orange, orange, green,

00:40:59.760 --> 00:41:02.040
and it's hard, right?

00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:06.540
And we feel bottlenecked
and there is a conflict.

00:41:06.540 --> 00:41:09.720
And here's a related
visual illusion to show you

00:41:09.720 --> 00:41:14.460
that we have limited
human attention resource.

00:41:14.460 --> 00:41:18.030
So in a change blindness example,

00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:21.510
I'm showing you two alternating pictures

00:41:21.510 --> 00:41:24.960
and there's one change in
the alternating pictures.

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:27.153
Nod your head when you see the change.

00:41:28.440 --> 00:41:31.320
Ooh, this is an IQ test.
(audience laughing)

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:36.300
Okay, so you didn't
nod as fast and as much

00:41:36.300 --> 00:41:40.950
as Molly Potter's detecting the person.

00:41:40.950 --> 00:41:43.503
So it's the jet engine.

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:47.820
Okay, let me do it again.
(audience chattering)

00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:51.540
Right? A huge part, right?

00:41:51.540 --> 00:41:54.420
And okay, so this is fun,

00:41:54.420 --> 00:41:57.810
but the limitation of
visual attention is not fun

00:41:57.810 --> 00:41:59.580
when it costs lives.

00:41:59.580 --> 00:42:04.020
In fact, medical errors
are the third leading cause

00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:07.530
of death in American healthcare system,

00:42:07.530 --> 00:42:11.770
and we have many situations
in the medical scenario

00:42:12.900 --> 00:42:15.030
that we need more visual attention.

00:42:15.030 --> 00:42:17.640
For example, surgical instrument,

00:42:17.640 --> 00:42:19.680
accounting for surgical instruments.

00:42:19.680 --> 00:42:22.170
There are so many of
them during a surgery,

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:25.800
and to this day, it's
humans, nurses and doctors,

00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:29.100
who have to tabulate and
count them during the process,

00:42:29.100 --> 00:42:33.960
and it slows down the surgery
and sometimes causes errors.

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:36.000
So can we imagine computers to help?

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:41.000
If they can recognize 2,800 types of cars

00:42:41.610 --> 00:42:45.990
or birds, can we account
for surgical instruments?

00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:48.150
Here is a pilot study we did

00:42:48.150 --> 00:42:52.260
in actual surgical rooms
at Stanford Hospital

00:42:52.260 --> 00:42:53.880
where the computer vision algorithm

00:42:53.880 --> 00:42:55.560
is automatically accounting

00:42:55.560 --> 00:42:57.767
for all the sponges in the scene,

00:42:57.767 --> 00:43:00.570
and we can imagine to take this further

00:43:00.570 --> 00:43:03.240
and help the doctors and nurses.

00:43:03.240 --> 00:43:06.810
So there is a lot more humans don't see

00:43:06.810 --> 00:43:10.650
that we can ask computers to see.

00:43:10.650 --> 00:43:13.950
But sometimes not seeing is more profound.

00:43:13.950 --> 00:43:17.130
This is really one of my
favorite visual illusion,

00:43:17.130 --> 00:43:19.080
and I'm just giving you
the answer right here.

00:43:19.080 --> 00:43:21.300
I didn't even have time to test you.

00:43:21.300 --> 00:43:25.107
So if you look at the top
image, square A and B,

00:43:25.107 --> 00:43:30.060
don't you swear they're of
different grayscale values?

00:43:30.060 --> 00:43:33.510
And then you copy this image
and put these two gray bars.

00:43:33.510 --> 00:43:36.240
They're identical gray values.

00:43:36.240 --> 00:43:37.770
Isn't that crazy?

00:43:37.770 --> 00:43:39.300
Because of the context,

00:43:39.300 --> 00:43:42.750
the prior knowledge of shape and lighting,

00:43:42.750 --> 00:43:44.550
you have a different way

00:43:44.550 --> 00:43:48.030
of processing this visual information.

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:50.530
For those of you who are
old enough, who are they?

00:43:51.812 --> 00:43:53.310
(audience laughing)

00:43:53.310 --> 00:43:54.570
Clinton and Gore, right?

00:43:54.570 --> 00:43:56.670
Of course it's Clinton and Gore.

00:43:56.670 --> 00:43:59.463
Really? Is it really Clinton and Gore?

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:05.253
Look hard. Now, it's
actually Clinton and Clinton.

00:44:06.420 --> 00:44:09.300
But because of your prior knowledge,

00:44:09.300 --> 00:44:12.810
the context, the hair and all this,

00:44:12.810 --> 00:44:15.570
you see two different people.

00:44:15.570 --> 00:44:19.620
You come with your own human visual bias.

00:44:19.620 --> 00:44:23.430
And this is a fun example,
but bias is not fun.

00:44:23.430 --> 00:44:26.850
Bias can be hurtful, bias can harm people

00:44:26.850 --> 00:44:29.580
and then can particularly harm people

00:44:29.580 --> 00:44:33.060
in underrepresented,
underserved community.

00:44:33.060 --> 00:44:38.060
So computer scientist
and poet Joy Buolamwini

00:44:38.130 --> 00:44:41.070
put this beautifully in a example

00:44:41.070 --> 00:44:45.750
in a poem she wrote called
"AI, Ain't I A Woman?"

00:44:45.750 --> 00:44:48.270
And this is to really raise the awareness

00:44:48.270 --> 00:44:50.460
of AI algorithms, in this case,

00:44:50.460 --> 00:44:52.440
facial recognition algorithms,

00:44:52.440 --> 00:44:55.680
failing to recognize women

00:44:55.680 --> 00:44:57.960
and people of color's faces.

00:44:57.960 --> 00:45:01.710
So AI can amplify human bias,

00:45:01.710 --> 00:45:04.830
especially if the data is biased

00:45:04.830 --> 00:45:08.730
and has issues.

00:45:08.730 --> 00:45:12.060
But the good news is there
is a growing body of work

00:45:12.060 --> 00:45:13.740
that is seeking to address this.

00:45:13.740 --> 00:45:16.140
I'm just showing you a few examples,

00:45:16.140 --> 00:45:18.240
and there's a lot more happening now

00:45:18.240 --> 00:45:22.020
and the awareness level is a lot higher.

00:45:22.020 --> 00:45:25.590
So we talk about bias, seen bias,

00:45:25.590 --> 00:45:29.550
but there is something even
more profound of not seeing,

00:45:29.550 --> 00:45:32.070
and it's very important.

00:45:32.070 --> 00:45:35.673
And consider the situations
when you don't want to be seen.

00:45:36.510 --> 00:45:39.420
In fact, it is really important

00:45:39.420 --> 00:45:43.317
to recognize privacy is
part of human dignity

00:45:43.317 --> 00:45:47.220
and it's part of human rights,
and when we are creating

00:45:47.220 --> 00:45:50.223
these powerful computer vision algorithms,

00:45:51.458 --> 00:45:54.780
how do we address the issue of privacy?

00:45:54.780 --> 00:45:56.790
I, for one, do not believe

00:45:56.790 --> 00:45:59.520
technology is the only solution

00:45:59.520 --> 00:46:02.670
to addressing human and societal issues,

00:46:02.670 --> 00:46:07.052
but technology must raise to
the location and participate

00:46:07.052 --> 00:46:10.620
in being a solution, not causing problems.

00:46:10.620 --> 00:46:13.902
So there are actually a growing number

00:46:13.902 --> 00:46:17.430
of technical approaches
to privacy computing

00:46:17.430 --> 00:46:18.750
in computer vision.

00:46:18.750 --> 00:46:22.590
Here I'm just listing a few,
whether it's face blurring,

00:46:22.590 --> 00:46:25.650
dimensionality reduction, body masking,

00:46:25.650 --> 00:46:28.800
federated learning,
homographic encryption.

00:46:28.800 --> 00:46:31.200
Some of you might not
know all these words,

00:46:31.200 --> 00:46:33.630
but for those of you who
come from computer science

00:46:33.630 --> 00:46:36.960
and data science, these
are just some of the work

00:46:36.960 --> 00:46:39.480
that my colleagues and I have worked in.

00:46:39.480 --> 00:46:42.360
And I want to just show you one work.

00:46:42.360 --> 00:46:43.830
It's actually not even my work.

00:46:43.830 --> 00:46:47.400
It's mostly my collaborator
Juan Carlos Niebles

00:46:47.400 --> 00:46:48.660
and his students.

00:46:48.660 --> 00:46:50.430
This is a really fun work

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:54.750
of doing privacy-aware
computer vision computing.

00:46:54.750 --> 00:46:56.850
They recognize that there is a need

00:46:56.850 --> 00:47:00.060
for computer vision technology
to recognize human gestures

00:47:00.060 --> 00:47:04.050
in many applications,
whether it's retail store

00:47:04.050 --> 00:47:07.260
or safety or a garage or whatever.

00:47:07.260 --> 00:47:10.530
But how do we do this in
a privacy-preserving way?

00:47:10.530 --> 00:47:14.370
They devised a lens

00:47:14.370 --> 00:47:17.700
that actually distorts the input.

00:47:17.700 --> 00:47:21.450
So instead of seeing humans, it blurs.

00:47:21.450 --> 00:47:24.330
The lens gives you images like this.

00:47:24.330 --> 00:47:27.720
But it's not enough to
just have a blurred picture

00:47:27.720 --> 00:47:30.240
because you still want to
recognize what's going on.

00:47:30.240 --> 00:47:34.500
So they actually developed
a set of algorithm

00:47:34.500 --> 00:47:36.690
that's coupled with this hardware

00:47:36.690 --> 00:47:41.690
so that even when the
pictures is distorted,

00:47:41.880 --> 00:47:43.740
the algorithm is still capable

00:47:43.740 --> 00:47:47.880
of recognizing relevant human activities.

00:47:47.880 --> 00:47:50.190
So this is a way to balance the need

00:47:50.190 --> 00:47:51.900
to recognize what's going on

00:47:51.900 --> 00:47:55.080
but to protect privacy
as much as possible.

00:47:55.080 --> 00:47:57.690
Of course, all this is just ongoing work

00:47:57.690 --> 00:48:01.320
and this is a long
journey, but in this part,

00:48:01.320 --> 00:48:04.920
we talk about building AI to
see what humans don't see,

00:48:04.920 --> 00:48:09.270
but what we really learned
is that bias, privacy,

00:48:09.270 --> 00:48:13.380
these are the issues AI
can amplify or exacerbate,

00:48:13.380 --> 00:48:17.700
many profound issues that
plagued humanity for ages.

00:48:17.700 --> 00:48:20.100
So we as technologist,

00:48:20.100 --> 00:48:24.060
along with other stakeholders, must commit

00:48:24.060 --> 00:48:27.480
to study, forecast and guide AI's impact

00:48:27.480 --> 00:48:31.710
on people and society in a
multidisciplinary approach.

00:48:31.710 --> 00:48:34.890
And these are some of the
lead collaborator students

00:48:34.890 --> 00:48:37.410
and postdocs who have contributed the work

00:48:37.410 --> 00:48:39.660
I talked about in this section.

00:48:39.660 --> 00:48:43.350
This brings us to the
last topic of the day.

00:48:43.350 --> 00:48:47.610
It's building AI to see
what humans want to see.

00:48:47.610 --> 00:48:48.450
Let's face it.

00:48:48.450 --> 00:48:50.883
When we talk about AI today,

00:48:51.870 --> 00:48:53.910
the most important concern,

00:48:53.910 --> 00:48:55.470
one of the most important concerns

00:48:55.470 --> 00:48:58.710
society has is the labor threat.

00:48:58.710 --> 00:49:01.830
You know, when we are
building automating machines,

00:49:01.830 --> 00:49:04.890
we're taking away human
jobs, be it truck drivers

00:49:04.890 --> 00:49:08.190
or radiologists or factory workers,

00:49:08.190 --> 00:49:11.670
and we see these kind of
headlines all the time.

00:49:11.670 --> 00:49:16.500
I for one have worked in a industry

00:49:16.500 --> 00:49:21.450
for the past 10 years that I see if not a,

00:49:21.450 --> 00:49:25.020
I see a different angle, and
I see a equally important

00:49:25.020 --> 00:49:28.500
or maybe even more
profoundly important problem

00:49:28.500 --> 00:49:31.530
is actually the current labor shortage

00:49:31.530 --> 00:49:34.470
in many of our industry,
such as healthcare.

00:49:34.470 --> 00:49:37.770
By next year, that's,
like, two month from now,

00:49:37.770 --> 00:49:40.860
America will be missing
one million nurses,

00:49:40.860 --> 00:49:43.530
and our current nurses are overworked

00:49:43.530 --> 00:49:46.620
and underpaid and over-exhausted.

00:49:46.620 --> 00:49:50.400
And these are the issues,
the very human issues,

00:49:50.400 --> 00:49:51.900
that we're facing.

00:49:51.900 --> 00:49:55.199
So how do we balance the concern

00:49:55.199 --> 00:49:59.310
for AI's labor threat, which is real

00:49:59.310 --> 00:50:01.500
and we need to really think about it,

00:50:01.500 --> 00:50:06.060
to many of the current
issues of labor shortage

00:50:06.060 --> 00:50:09.540
or even unsafe work environments?

00:50:09.540 --> 00:50:12.690
So we really believe,
and I really believe,

00:50:12.690 --> 00:50:14.670
there is a verb we need to replace,

00:50:14.670 --> 00:50:17.010
and it's the word replace itself.

00:50:17.010 --> 00:50:21.600
AI should be augmenting
human capabilities,

00:50:21.600 --> 00:50:24.300
not replacing human capabilities.

00:50:24.300 --> 00:50:27.540
And I want to show you two lines of work

00:50:27.540 --> 00:50:31.530
in this arguing for AI's augmentation.

00:50:31.530 --> 00:50:33.510
One is backing healthcare.

00:50:33.510 --> 00:50:36.510
I already give you a little
bit of a teaser early on

00:50:36.510 --> 00:50:40.110
that medical error is a
leading cause of death,

00:50:40.110 --> 00:50:41.520
one of the leading cause of death

00:50:41.520 --> 00:50:43.560
in American healthcare system.

00:50:43.560 --> 00:50:46.320
In fact, there are many
healthcare scenarios

00:50:46.320 --> 00:50:49.620
where we don't have
enough human eyes to see,

00:50:49.620 --> 00:50:53.190
to ensure quality, and to ensure safety.

00:50:53.190 --> 00:50:55.590
So more than 10 years ago,

00:50:55.590 --> 00:50:58.770
my colleagues in Stanford's medical school

00:50:58.770 --> 00:51:02.640
and I came together and
start to really imagine,

00:51:02.640 --> 00:51:06.120
can we, in this new wave
of machine learning,

00:51:06.120 --> 00:51:09.330
deep learning revolution,
and smart sensors,

00:51:09.330 --> 00:51:13.590
can we imagine that we
use these smart sensors

00:51:13.590 --> 00:51:18.150
and ML algorithms to glean
health-critical insights

00:51:18.150 --> 00:51:21.210
that are important for
patients and clinicians?

00:51:21.210 --> 00:51:23.700
And this technology is what we call

00:51:23.700 --> 00:51:26.130
ambient intelligence for healthcare.

00:51:26.130 --> 00:51:28.710
A couple of years ago, we put our work,

00:51:28.710 --> 00:51:31.140
10 years' work, in a "Nature" review.

00:51:31.140 --> 00:51:33.780
I'm not going to get into
the details of this paper,

00:51:33.780 --> 00:51:36.750
but I'll give you a a couple of nuggets

00:51:36.750 --> 00:51:38.400
of what we talked about.

00:51:38.400 --> 00:51:40.530
The first one is hand hygiene.

00:51:40.530 --> 00:51:43.650
Hand hygiene practice,
proper hand hygiene practice,

00:51:43.650 --> 00:51:47.400
is very important to reduce
hospital-acquired infection,

00:51:47.400 --> 00:51:51.270
which causes tens of thousands of lives,

00:51:51.270 --> 00:51:55.200
three times more than car
accidents on Americans' road,

00:51:55.200 --> 00:51:58.170
and whether we're talking
about human auditors

00:51:58.170 --> 00:52:00.360
or technology like RFID,

00:52:00.360 --> 00:52:03.360
they are actually more or less ineffective

00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:08.360
or too time- and labor-prohibitive.

00:52:08.820 --> 00:52:12.630
So we piloted this project
at Stanford Hospital

00:52:12.630 --> 00:52:16.620
where we put a smart sensor
on top of the gel dispenser,

00:52:16.620 --> 00:52:18.840
the sanitization stations,

00:52:18.840 --> 00:52:21.210
and these sensors capture depth images

00:52:21.210 --> 00:52:23.010
that look like the blue videos.

00:52:23.010 --> 00:52:26.130
So it's more or less
protecting the privacy

00:52:26.130 --> 00:52:28.890
of our clinicians, but in the meantime,

00:52:28.890 --> 00:52:33.210
our backend algorithms is running 24/7,

00:52:33.210 --> 00:52:36.810
recognizing proper hand
hygiene activities.

00:52:36.810 --> 00:52:39.750
Here, if you see a green box,

00:52:39.750 --> 00:52:43.170
it means the clinician is
properly gelling his or her hand

00:52:43.170 --> 00:52:47.220
before entering the patient
room, and the red box shows

00:52:47.220 --> 00:52:50.880
that proper hand hygiene
movement did not happen.

00:52:50.880 --> 00:52:55.290
Our algorithm performs
on par with ground truth,

00:52:55.290 --> 00:52:57.330
is a lot better than one, three,

00:52:57.330 --> 00:53:00.510
or even four human observers.

00:53:00.510 --> 00:53:04.860
So we also worked in ICUs.

00:53:04.860 --> 00:53:09.300
Patient mobility is linked
to improved recovery,

00:53:09.300 --> 00:53:12.360
but how do we know a patient
is being moved properly,

00:53:12.360 --> 00:53:15.960
both in terms of frequency
as well as other measures?

00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:16.980
It's hard.

00:53:16.980 --> 00:53:19.680
We're still resorting on human auditors,

00:53:19.680 --> 00:53:24.540
and if you know anything about
the work of ICU clinicians,

00:53:24.540 --> 00:53:28.890
you know they're overworked
and there are not enough hours

00:53:28.890 --> 00:53:31.350
to even cover how much they need to do.

00:53:31.350 --> 00:53:34.380
So again, we put these smart sensors

00:53:34.380 --> 00:53:37.560
in Stanford Hospital

00:53:37.560 --> 00:53:39.810
as well as a hospital in Utah

00:53:39.810 --> 00:53:42.870
and start working with clinicians

00:53:42.870 --> 00:53:47.850
on can we prototype
potential AI help here.

00:53:47.850 --> 00:53:49.200
So here, you're seeing

00:53:49.200 --> 00:53:54.200
that our AI algorithm is
recognizing four types

00:53:54.510 --> 00:53:59.490
of activities or mobility
movements in the hospital,

00:53:59.490 --> 00:54:01.140
getting out of bed, getting in bed,

00:54:01.140 --> 00:54:03.330
getting out of chair,
and getting in chair.

00:54:03.330 --> 00:54:07.860
And we're showing that our
algorithm is performing very well

00:54:07.860 --> 00:54:10.590
against human-labeled ground truth.

00:54:10.590 --> 00:54:14.520
Last but not the least,
there is a lot more potential

00:54:14.520 --> 00:54:17.670
in using this kind of ambient
intelligence technology

00:54:17.670 --> 00:54:20.430
in senior homes and aging in place

00:54:20.430 --> 00:54:23.700
to predict and prevent unsafe events,

00:54:23.700 --> 00:54:26.250
monitor patients with mild symptoms,

00:54:26.250 --> 00:54:28.300
and manage chronic conditions

00:54:29.250 --> 00:54:34.250
such as infection, mobility,
sleep patterns, and diet.

00:54:34.560 --> 00:54:38.850
In fact, I pay particular
attention to this aging issue

00:54:38.850 --> 00:54:42.420
because America is aging,
the world is aging,

00:54:42.420 --> 00:54:47.420
and we're having a real
labor shortage of caretakers.

00:54:47.430 --> 00:54:52.430
So can we go a step beyond
just passive smart sensors

00:54:52.590 --> 00:54:54.660
to send alerts to doctors

00:54:54.660 --> 00:54:57.420
and families when it comes to aging?

00:54:57.420 --> 00:54:59.103
Here's a little bit of sci-fi.

00:55:00.090 --> 00:55:02.550
Because of the decrease of,

00:55:02.550 --> 00:55:06.360
you know, availability of caregivers,

00:55:06.360 --> 00:55:08.370
can we imagine one day AI

00:55:08.370 --> 00:55:10.680
in the forms of robots

00:55:10.680 --> 00:55:14.550
can also help our seniors

00:55:14.550 --> 00:55:16.620
or aging in place?

00:55:16.620 --> 00:55:20.370
And here is not to replace humans,

00:55:20.370 --> 00:55:22.440
human connection, human emotion.

00:55:22.440 --> 00:55:23.880
Family is important.

00:55:23.880 --> 00:55:27.930
But there is a lot of tasks
we can imagine robots to do.

00:55:27.930 --> 00:55:32.420
And it's also, remember,
there is 18.4 billion hours

00:55:32.420 --> 00:55:36.990
of home assistance from unpaid caregivers

00:55:36.990 --> 00:55:40.650
that's not accounted in our current GDP.

00:55:40.650 --> 00:55:43.140
And guess who it impacts the most?

00:55:43.140 --> 00:55:45.090
Women and people of color.

00:55:45.090 --> 00:55:48.270
So as a AI scientist,

00:55:48.270 --> 00:55:52.590
I'm extremely excited to
imagine a robotic future

00:55:52.590 --> 00:55:56.190
that can help people at home.

00:55:56.190 --> 00:55:59.640
Well, don't get too excited too soon.

00:55:59.640 --> 00:56:02.220
Here's where today's robots are.

00:56:02.220 --> 00:56:06.300
We hear a lot of robots
in factory settings.

00:56:06.300 --> 00:56:10.470
Those are very structured
and pre-programed settings.

00:56:10.470 --> 00:56:14.850
If we put a robot in a
unstructured setting like this,

00:56:14.850 --> 00:56:18.960
the poor guy can barely
put a box on a cart.

00:56:18.960 --> 00:56:21.060
And this is...

00:56:21.060 --> 00:56:25.800
I hope I didn't offend any
roboticists in the audience.

00:56:25.800 --> 00:56:30.120
We know that there's a
long way to go in robotics.

00:56:30.120 --> 00:56:34.320
In fact, most of robotic research today

00:56:34.320 --> 00:56:38.310
is still more or less limited
to short-horizon tasks

00:56:38.310 --> 00:56:43.050
such as placing something
or opening a drawer

00:56:43.050 --> 00:56:48.050
or door or inserting a peg in a hole.

00:56:48.210 --> 00:56:52.170
And moreover, if you
look at robotic research

00:56:52.170 --> 00:56:56.160
and the papers we publish,
they're mostly done,

00:56:56.160 --> 00:56:59.730
our experiments, in artificially
simple environments,

00:56:59.730 --> 00:57:04.410
small-scale and anecdotal
experimenter-picked tasks

00:57:04.410 --> 00:57:08.670
and setup, lacks standard
benchmark and metrics.

00:57:08.670 --> 00:57:12.570
And if we want to connect this to the goal

00:57:12.570 --> 00:57:17.370
of everyday robot, the real
world is a lot more complex,

00:57:17.370 --> 00:57:21.180
dynamic, and uncertain,
has large variabilities,

00:57:21.180 --> 00:57:25.260
interactive and social,
and has a lot of tasks.

00:57:25.260 --> 00:57:28.230
So how do we close this gap?

00:57:28.230 --> 00:57:29.910
Well, if we have learned anything

00:57:29.910 --> 00:57:34.140
in the past decades of the
deep learning revolution,

00:57:34.140 --> 00:57:37.900
we have learned that data and benchmark

00:57:39.298 --> 00:57:42.090
has come a long way in
other related fields,

00:57:42.090 --> 00:57:46.911
such as computer vision and
natural language processing.

00:57:46.911 --> 00:57:50.286
In fact, with this kind of inspiration,

00:57:50.286 --> 00:57:54.300
we ask ourself, can we get inspired

00:57:54.300 --> 00:57:59.100
for a new north star for
robotic learning and embody AI?

00:57:59.100 --> 00:58:00.810
And this is the last work I want

00:58:00.810 --> 00:58:03.150
to present in this lecture today,

00:58:03.150 --> 00:58:06.810
is a ecological robotic
learning environment

00:58:06.810 --> 00:58:11.320
and a large and diverse set of activities

00:58:12.630 --> 00:58:17.490
that is proposed by the
BEHAVIOR Project at Stanford,

00:58:17.490 --> 00:58:21.660
which is a benchmark for
everyday household activities

00:58:21.660 --> 00:58:25.560
in virtual interactive
ecological environments.

00:58:25.560 --> 00:58:27.570
It's been so fun to work on this project

00:58:27.570 --> 00:58:28.617
in the past three years,

00:58:28.617 --> 00:58:31.260
and we have both cognitive psychologists

00:58:31.260 --> 00:58:35.493
and roboticists and computer
scientists working together.

00:58:36.450 --> 00:58:40.020
Before, we're talking about,
oh, wow, this is virtual world.

00:58:40.020 --> 00:58:41.430
What are they talking about?

00:58:41.430 --> 00:58:43.770
Let's just take a moment and think.

00:58:43.770 --> 00:58:45.360
At the end of the day,

00:58:45.360 --> 00:58:48.990
what kind of tasks are
we talking about here?

00:58:48.990 --> 00:58:53.400
You want robot to help
everyday household activities.

00:58:53.400 --> 00:58:56.400
What are the everyday
household activities?

00:58:56.400 --> 00:58:59.373
Well, let's do a little bit
of a experiment right here.

00:59:00.210 --> 00:59:04.096
I'm going to ask you about
some household activities

00:59:04.096 --> 00:59:07.620
that you would like a
robot to help you with.

00:59:07.620 --> 00:59:11.670
Just, like, nod or say
something if you like that.

00:59:11.670 --> 00:59:14.430
What about cleaning the kitchen floor?

00:59:14.430 --> 00:59:15.720
Okay, good.

00:59:15.720 --> 00:59:17.490
Yeah. Good.

00:59:17.490 --> 00:59:20.010
Shoveling snow in Connecticut?

00:59:20.010 --> 00:59:22.140
Okay, good, good, good.

00:59:22.140 --> 00:59:24.023
Folding laundry? Okay.

00:59:25.410 --> 00:59:27.270
You know, I fold a lot of laundry at home.

00:59:27.270 --> 00:59:28.860
I actually like that time.

00:59:28.860 --> 00:59:31.530
But anyway, robot can do that.

00:59:31.530 --> 00:59:32.943
Cooking breakfast?

00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:38.520
I'm getting a little
bit of a pause from you.

00:59:38.520 --> 00:59:40.380
Okay, this last one is the best.

00:59:40.380 --> 00:59:43.602
Opening Christmas gifts?
(audience laughing)

00:59:43.602 --> 00:59:44.550
Okay.

00:59:44.550 --> 00:59:47.370
By the way, I don't know if
you notice, every single one

00:59:47.370 --> 00:59:52.370
of the images are AI generated
here, not human artists, so.

00:59:52.453 --> 00:59:54.810
Okay, so that's good.

00:59:54.810 --> 00:59:57.300
So this is really important.

00:59:57.300 --> 01:00:00.030
We are very enthusiastic technologist,

01:00:00.030 --> 01:00:02.130
but before we even start our project,

01:00:02.130 --> 01:00:06.330
let's focus on humans and
people and people's need.

01:00:06.330 --> 01:00:08.790
So before we started BEHAVIOR Project,

01:00:08.790 --> 01:00:11.700
we actually did a large-scale user study

01:00:11.700 --> 01:00:16.680
with 1,400 participants
from Amazon Mechanical Turk

01:00:16.680 --> 01:00:20.520
and screened and curated

01:00:20.520 --> 01:00:23.940
2,000-plus everyday activities

01:00:23.940 --> 01:00:26.610
that are proposed by government data

01:00:26.610 --> 01:00:30.270
and ask people what would they,

01:00:30.270 --> 01:00:34.080
how much would they benefit
if a robot did this for them?

01:00:34.080 --> 01:00:36.630
Because it's important
to know what humans need.

01:00:36.630 --> 01:00:38.190
And then we got the scores.

01:00:38.190 --> 01:00:39.360
We ranked them.

01:00:39.360 --> 01:00:41.160
As you just showed,
(audience laughing)

01:00:41.160 --> 01:00:43.743
cleaning, we all want cleaning.

01:00:46.110 --> 01:00:48.510
But people don't like robots

01:00:48.510 --> 01:00:52.710
to open Christmas gift or throw darts.

01:00:52.710 --> 01:00:55.203
I guess they're worry robot would win.

01:00:56.520 --> 01:00:58.080
Or buy a ring.

01:00:58.080 --> 01:01:00.060
I hope not. So, okay.

01:01:00.060 --> 01:01:03.510
So if you put the top 1,000 tasks

01:01:03.510 --> 01:01:05.850
that people prefer robots help,

01:01:05.850 --> 01:01:09.720
they actually do cluster
into mostly cleaning tasks

01:01:09.720 --> 01:01:12.270
or some simple cooking tasks

01:01:12.270 --> 01:01:15.780
or some boring shopping tasks, and so...

01:01:15.780 --> 01:01:16.613
So that's good.

01:01:16.613 --> 01:01:18.450
So we decided we're going

01:01:18.450 --> 01:01:22.260
to take the top-ranked
1,000 household activities

01:01:22.260 --> 01:01:27.260
as the baseline or as the
activities we will work towards

01:01:27.570 --> 01:01:30.150
for our robotic learning project.

01:01:30.150 --> 01:01:31.470
But that's not enough.

01:01:31.470 --> 01:01:33.030
In what environment?

01:01:33.030 --> 01:01:37.110
We don't want to use
anecdotal, small-scale,

01:01:37.110 --> 01:01:39.990
simple laboratory-made environment.

01:01:39.990 --> 01:01:44.290
So we actually scanned 50
real-world environments

01:01:45.480 --> 01:01:49.260
from apartments to restaurants to offices

01:01:49.260 --> 01:01:53.130
to stores and use these as basis

01:01:53.130 --> 01:01:57.390
for building our robotic training world,

01:01:57.390 --> 01:02:02.390
and we also obtained
5,000 3D object models

01:02:02.520 --> 01:02:05.910
across 1,200 object categories,

01:02:05.910 --> 01:02:08.490
and these object asset models

01:02:08.490 --> 01:02:11.550
cover 30-plus different properties from,

01:02:11.550 --> 01:02:14.760
you know, temperature
changes to articulation

01:02:14.760 --> 01:02:17.700
and deformability and so on.

01:02:17.700 --> 01:02:22.140
Now with all these ingredients
together, we're ready to make

01:02:22.140 --> 01:02:26.220
a unprecedented large-scale
simulation environment

01:02:26.220 --> 01:02:28.380
for robotic learning.

01:02:28.380 --> 01:02:31.110
Simulation environments are very critical.

01:02:31.110 --> 01:02:33.240
In fact, they're fast,

01:02:33.240 --> 01:02:35.820
they can be used to
transfer to real world,

01:02:35.820 --> 01:02:39.570
they're safe, and they
are more reproducible

01:02:39.570 --> 01:02:42.780
and we can work towards
a more fair data set

01:02:42.780 --> 01:02:46.170
using simulation, and
there has been good work.

01:02:46.170 --> 01:02:50.070
There has been a lot of
efforts in the field of AI

01:02:50.070 --> 01:02:52.530
in creating simulation environments.

01:02:52.530 --> 01:02:53.460
And these are some

01:02:53.460 --> 01:02:55.980
of the state-of-the-art
simulation environments

01:02:55.980 --> 01:02:58.260
by our colleagues in the world.

01:02:58.260 --> 01:03:01.290
BEHAVIOR Project, we collaborated,

01:03:01.290 --> 01:03:05.070
we partnered with NVIDIA's Omniverse team,

01:03:05.070 --> 01:03:08.910
which is a commercial-grade
simulation environment,

01:03:08.910 --> 01:03:13.260
and we aimed at creating
a realistic environment

01:03:13.260 --> 01:03:15.687
to train robots both in physics,

01:03:15.687 --> 01:03:17.910
perception, and interaction.

01:03:17.910 --> 01:03:21.300
Here I'm showing you examples
of realistic physics.

01:03:21.300 --> 01:03:23.220
You see thermal effects.

01:03:23.220 --> 01:03:25.650
You see lighting and reflection effects.

01:03:25.650 --> 01:03:29.520
You see fluid,
deformability, transparency.

01:03:29.520 --> 01:03:34.080
We also did a user study on
the realism for perception.

01:03:34.080 --> 01:03:37.320
We compared BEHAVIOR's environment

01:03:37.320 --> 01:03:39.000
or simulation environment

01:03:39.000 --> 01:03:41.070
against other simulation environment

01:03:41.070 --> 01:03:45.660
and ask people to score
the perceptual realism

01:03:45.660 --> 01:03:48.450
and BEHAVIOR came out pretty well.

01:03:48.450 --> 01:03:53.450
We also try very hard to
reproduce the real physical,

01:03:53.490 --> 01:03:55.590
the realism in physical interaction.

01:03:55.590 --> 01:03:59.280
Here you're seeing robots

01:03:59.280 --> 01:04:02.370
doing things with pretty complex materials

01:04:02.370 --> 01:04:06.810
such as water, fluids,
or deformable materials.

01:04:06.810 --> 01:04:10.170
So this is just a very messy, nerdy chart

01:04:10.170 --> 01:04:12.000
that we put together to show you

01:04:12.000 --> 01:04:15.480
the quantitative comparisons of BEHAVIOR

01:04:15.480 --> 01:04:17.760
compared to other simulation environment

01:04:17.760 --> 01:04:22.500
and how BEHAVIOR is more diverse,

01:04:22.500 --> 01:04:25.050
more large scale and complex

01:04:25.050 --> 01:04:27.840
in several important dimensions.

01:04:27.840 --> 01:04:29.130
Of course, with the goal

01:04:29.130 --> 01:04:33.480
of creating a new generation
of robotic algorithms

01:04:33.480 --> 01:04:36.120
for these everyday household activities,

01:04:36.120 --> 01:04:38.010
we want to start by asking the question,

01:04:38.010 --> 01:04:40.350
how did today's algorithm do?

01:04:40.350 --> 01:04:42.900
We picked three BEHAVIOR activities,

01:04:42.900 --> 01:04:47.900
store decoration, collecting
trash, and cleaning table,

01:04:48.270 --> 01:04:52.410
to benchmark against today's
state-of-the-art algorithm,

01:04:52.410 --> 01:04:53.640
this is a little dense,

01:04:53.640 --> 01:04:56.550
I promise this is the last
dense part of the talk,

01:04:56.550 --> 01:05:00.420
where we use these three tasks

01:05:00.420 --> 01:05:04.170
and we change different
conditions of the algorithm

01:05:04.170 --> 01:05:07.350
to see how the
state-of-the-art algorithm do.

01:05:07.350 --> 01:05:10.020
Let me just say that if we do not give

01:05:10.020 --> 01:05:12.652
artificially privileged information

01:05:12.652 --> 01:05:15.990
to today's state-of-the-art algorithms,

01:05:15.990 --> 01:05:17.553
the performance is zero.

01:05:19.140 --> 01:05:20.880
Well, that's actually encouraging,

01:05:20.880 --> 01:05:22.980
at least that's what
I said to my students,

01:05:22.980 --> 01:05:25.470
is that you want to be challenging

01:05:25.470 --> 01:05:27.420
and this is where we can make progress.

01:05:28.800 --> 01:05:31.350
But of course if we relax

01:05:31.350 --> 01:05:34.710
and give some privileged
information, for example,

01:05:34.710 --> 01:05:37.920
here, we give it what we
call action primitives,

01:05:37.920 --> 01:05:40.770
which is, for those of
you coming from robotics,

01:05:40.770 --> 01:05:42.690
you'll know this is a little more,

01:05:42.690 --> 01:05:45.690
just pre-programed movements,

01:05:45.690 --> 01:05:48.210
then the performance start to improve,

01:05:48.210 --> 01:05:51.450
and if you add some
artificial memory to this,

01:05:51.450 --> 01:05:53.430
it'll improve even more.

01:05:53.430 --> 01:05:55.380
All this is to say,

01:05:55.380 --> 01:05:59.370
this heavy chart is to say,

01:05:59.370 --> 01:06:02.040
that BEHAVIOR is perhaps one

01:06:02.040 --> 01:06:04.470
of the most challenging benchmark

01:06:04.470 --> 01:06:06.450
in today's robotic learning

01:06:06.450 --> 01:06:08.160
and we find it very exciting

01:06:08.160 --> 01:06:11.430
that we have now this fertile playground

01:06:11.430 --> 01:06:14.790
to train 1,000 household activities

01:06:14.790 --> 01:06:17.190
for robotic learning algorithms.

01:06:17.190 --> 01:06:18.990
And we're not stopping here.

01:06:18.990 --> 01:06:22.980
We're also taking
simulation into real world

01:06:22.980 --> 01:06:27.420
and creating a Sim2Real possibility.

01:06:27.420 --> 01:06:31.140
At Stanford, we created
a real-world apartment

01:06:31.140 --> 01:06:34.830
and also have a real-world
robot called Marvin

01:06:34.830 --> 01:06:38.640
working with its own digital twin

01:06:38.640 --> 01:06:40.380
in the simulation environment

01:06:40.380 --> 01:06:44.580
so that we are simultaneously
training the virtual agent

01:06:44.580 --> 01:06:46.590
as well as the physical agent.

01:06:46.590 --> 01:06:48.213
Here's actually Marvin.

01:06:49.380 --> 01:06:52.680
Marvin's very slow,
(audience laughing)

01:06:52.680 --> 01:06:55.230
but Marvin is actually successfully going

01:06:55.230 --> 01:06:58.470
to pick up this bottle with the goal

01:06:58.470 --> 01:07:01.260
of sending this bottle to a trash bin.

01:07:01.260 --> 01:07:03.540
So let's see if Marvin does that.

01:07:03.540 --> 01:07:05.073
So please bear with me.

01:07:06.780 --> 01:07:10.410
Marvin in this case
successfully will navigate

01:07:10.410 --> 01:07:14.070
to the trash bin and place
the bottle inside, okay?

01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:18.450
So but Marvin is not
successful in many, many times.

01:07:18.450 --> 01:07:21.930
Sometimes, it navigate to the wrong table.

01:07:21.930 --> 01:07:25.923
Sometimes, it just doesn't
pick up the bottle.

01:07:27.090 --> 01:07:31.860
Sometimes, it didn't manage

01:07:31.860 --> 01:07:35.130
to place the cup in the right place.

01:07:35.130 --> 01:07:37.740
So I'm just showing you this to show you

01:07:37.740 --> 01:07:40.557
we still have a long way
to go to get the, you know,

01:07:40.557 --> 01:07:44.193
the cleaning kitchen
floor thing going for us.

01:07:45.570 --> 01:07:49.650
And this is a teaser video to
show you our BEHAVIOR Project

01:07:49.650 --> 01:07:50.910
and give you a little more sense

01:07:50.910 --> 01:07:53.733
of the diversity of this environment.

01:07:54.960 --> 01:07:57.870
All these are our simulation environments,

01:07:57.870 --> 01:08:01.660
and with the video, you get
to appreciate a little more

01:08:02.754 --> 01:08:05.640
of the complexity of BEHAVIOR,

01:08:05.640 --> 01:08:08.280
complexity of the environments,

01:08:08.280 --> 01:08:13.110
the large-scale things
from store to apartments

01:08:13.110 --> 01:08:17.913
to restaurants, the kind
of different objects,

01:08:19.590 --> 01:08:23.193
and of course the website of BEHAVIOR.

01:08:24.540 --> 01:08:29.340
Okay, so we started by that per robot

01:08:29.340 --> 01:08:32.940
and our goal is one day,
we'll create household robot.

01:08:32.940 --> 01:08:34.380
And that's still a long journey,

01:08:34.380 --> 01:08:36.120
but I'm very excited by BEHAVIOR

01:08:36.120 --> 01:08:38.730
because it is born with the goal

01:08:38.730 --> 01:08:41.400
of augmenting people,
not to replace people.

01:08:41.400 --> 01:08:43.800
It's large scale and diverse

01:08:43.800 --> 01:08:46.260
and it's striving

01:08:46.260 --> 01:08:48.990
to be realistic and ecological.

01:08:48.990 --> 01:08:52.080
So with all this work in building AI

01:08:52.080 --> 01:08:57.080
to see and do what humans want
to see or want them to do,

01:08:57.270 --> 01:09:00.300
we learn the value that AI must seek

01:09:00.300 --> 01:09:03.750
to augment, not replace, our individual

01:09:03.750 --> 01:09:08.100
and collective human
capabilities and wellbeing,

01:09:08.100 --> 01:09:11.430
and there are a lot of
collaborator, students,

01:09:11.430 --> 01:09:15.930
and postdocs who have contributed
to this part of the work.

01:09:15.930 --> 01:09:19.893
So this brings us almost to
the closure of this lecture.

01:09:21.009 --> 01:09:24.840
We've talked about three kinds of AI work

01:09:24.840 --> 01:09:26.970
we have done in our lab.

01:09:26.970 --> 01:09:31.230
What's most important is
after this 20-year journey

01:09:31.230 --> 01:09:35.250
in building AI, we have
actually learned a lot

01:09:35.250 --> 01:09:40.200
of human lessons, that the
development of AI must be guided

01:09:40.200 --> 01:09:42.810
by the concern for its human impact,

01:09:42.810 --> 01:09:46.530
that AI should strive to
augment and enhance humans,

01:09:46.530 --> 01:09:51.180
not replace humans, and that
AI technologies must continue

01:09:51.180 --> 01:09:55.500
to be inspired by human
intelligence and brain sciences.

01:09:55.500 --> 01:09:59.310
And with these three
fundamental human values,

01:09:59.310 --> 01:10:02.010
Stanford launched three years ago

01:10:02.010 --> 01:10:04.530
the Human-Centered AI Institute

01:10:04.530 --> 01:10:08.850
with the support of a
broad faculty leadership

01:10:08.850 --> 01:10:11.850
across our, at that time, seven schools,

01:10:11.850 --> 01:10:13.980
now we have eight schools,

01:10:13.980 --> 01:10:17.880
from law to business to
medicine to humanities

01:10:17.880 --> 01:10:21.750
to engineering and natural sciences.

01:10:21.750 --> 01:10:25.350
And HAI is a very young institute.

01:10:25.350 --> 01:10:28.320
I'll just highlight a couple
of things we have done,

01:10:28.320 --> 01:10:30.960
mostly during pandemic, I guess.

01:10:30.960 --> 01:10:33.930
We have launched the digital economy lab

01:10:33.930 --> 01:10:37.080
to specifically put together economist

01:10:37.080 --> 01:10:39.480
and computer scientist and technologist

01:10:39.480 --> 01:10:42.870
to study the impact of
society, of labor market,

01:10:42.870 --> 01:10:47.070
of our economy in this digital age.

01:10:47.070 --> 01:10:51.690
We have launched a Center for
Research on Foundation Models.

01:10:51.690 --> 01:10:55.350
All the news-generating AI you see today,

01:10:55.350 --> 01:10:58.830
whether it's GPT-3 or this DALL-E art,

01:10:58.830 --> 01:11:02.130
is by these large foundation models,

01:11:02.130 --> 01:11:05.430
and they have profound
implication in our industry

01:11:05.430 --> 01:11:07.410
as well as AI application,

01:11:07.410 --> 01:11:10.230
both from ethical societal point of view

01:11:10.230 --> 01:11:12.690
as well as from technology point of view.

01:11:12.690 --> 01:11:14.880
So we have professors, faculty,

01:11:14.880 --> 01:11:17.700
researchers coming together to study this.

01:11:17.700 --> 01:11:19.740
We also pioneered a new way

01:11:19.740 --> 01:11:22.860
of reviewing AI research grants

01:11:22.860 --> 01:11:25.050
called Ethics and Society Review

01:11:25.050 --> 01:11:28.920
inspired by the human subject IRB review

01:11:28.920 --> 01:11:31.740
led by political scientist, a humanist,

01:11:31.740 --> 01:11:33.720
and a computer scientist.

01:11:33.720 --> 01:11:35.934
We also have education program

01:11:35.934 --> 01:11:39.090
geared towards undergraduates
and graduate students

01:11:39.090 --> 01:11:43.590
by embedding ethics in our
computer science classes.

01:11:43.590 --> 01:11:48.590
But our education didn't stop
within the walls of Stanford.

01:11:48.630 --> 01:11:51.420
We reached out beyond
the walls of Stanford

01:11:51.420 --> 01:11:56.220
and start to extend AI education
to professional audiences

01:11:56.220 --> 01:11:59.583
like business executives, journalists,

01:12:01.140 --> 01:12:04.950
lawyers, and judges as
well as policy makers,

01:12:04.950 --> 01:12:06.690
especially in Washington DC

01:12:06.690 --> 01:12:09.330
but also at the state and local level.

01:12:09.330 --> 01:12:12.060
And Stanford was very proud

01:12:12.060 --> 01:12:15.360
to be leading the successful lobbying

01:12:15.360 --> 01:12:19.590
of a bill called National
AI Research Resource,

01:12:19.590 --> 01:12:24.270
and I believe Yale was part of the team,

01:12:24.270 --> 01:12:28.170
and thank you for the successful effort.

01:12:28.170 --> 01:12:30.300
So Biden administration launched

01:12:30.300 --> 01:12:33.180
the National Artificial
Intelligence Research Resource

01:12:33.180 --> 01:12:34.950
task force two years ago.

01:12:34.950 --> 01:12:36.420
I'm very honored and humbled

01:12:36.420 --> 01:12:39.330
to be one of the 12-people task force,

01:12:39.330 --> 01:12:42.540
and in a few weeks we'll
be presenting to Congress

01:12:42.540 --> 01:12:47.540
this new idea of how to
rejuvenate America's public

01:12:48.120 --> 01:12:51.330
and higher education sectors capability

01:12:51.330 --> 01:12:56.190
of innovating and education in AI.

01:12:56.190 --> 01:12:59.850
So all in all, it has been an exhilarating

01:12:59.850 --> 01:13:03.309
as well as humbling journey to create AI

01:13:03.309 --> 01:13:06.150
to enable machines to see,

01:13:06.150 --> 01:13:09.210
to realize the audacious ambition

01:13:09.210 --> 01:13:14.070
that evolution have shown
us for 540 million years

01:13:14.070 --> 01:13:17.730
to create intelligent machines in silicon.

01:13:17.730 --> 01:13:22.730
But it's incredible that
along this technical journey,

01:13:22.950 --> 01:13:25.410
we've learned more human lessons

01:13:25.410 --> 01:13:28.417
and understand more human values

01:13:28.417 --> 01:13:32.850
than machine-learned lessons,
and this is continue.

01:13:32.850 --> 01:13:35.100
This is only in the
beginning of the journey,

01:13:35.100 --> 01:13:37.620
and I want to thank, of
course, all my students

01:13:37.620 --> 01:13:42.090
and collaborators and also sponsors

01:13:42.090 --> 01:13:44.370
to support this research.

01:13:44.370 --> 01:13:46.237
And thank you for listening.

01:13:46.237 --> 01:13:49.404
(audience applauding)

01:14:06.522 --> 01:14:08.939
(soft music)

